\f0\fs36 \cf0 User-Definable Keys\
\
Kind of a misnomer 
\f1\i (see scriv notes)
\f0\i0 .  These keys act like modern function keys - press an F-key to get some behavior, potentially interrupting the current program to handle them.  The user has 20 of these on the keyboard - ten physical keys, plus the shift modifier\
\

\f1\i (insert picture of the keys)
\f0\i0 \
\
When you type one of the user-definable keys, you tell the program to finish whatever instruction it is currently running, then GOSUB a particular line of code.  The line number to jump to is calculated from the key number by multiplying it by four.  Press UDKey number one, that jumps to line four.  Press UDKey number 13 (shift-3), and it jumps to line 52.  \
\

\f1\i (insert illustration of the "jump table")\

\f0\i0 \
You have four lines of code to do you work, and you are expected terminate your work with an END, STOP, or RETURN.  END to gracefully end your program (and close open files), STOP to generate a visible error message to the user, or RETURN to tell BASIC to pick up where it left off.\
\
This isn't enforced.  Line 52 can happily continue on to line 56 (handler for UDKey 14), but this might not be what you expect.  If line 56 is actually 
\f1\i en medias res
\f0\i0  for UDKey 13's handler, the user pressing UDKey 14 would jump into the middle of that, perhaps leading to hilarity (or mayhem).  Or maybe you can do something clever with UDKey 13 setting up some specific condition that a more general handler for UDKey 14 could use. (Similar to the logic of a fall-through branch in a C-style switch statement.)\
